# Credentials for mrglass, the merge-request dashboard (~/projects/mrglass),
# reached through the mrs / mrsw / mrsd / mrsn aliases in zsh/.zshrc.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/mrglass/secrets.env (mode 600). install.sh does that copy
# for you on a fresh machine. THIS FILE IS THE TEMPLATE AND IS COMMITTED; the
# filled-in copy lives outside the repo and must never enter git.
#
# zsh/.zshenv sources the real file for EVERY zsh invocation, including
# non-interactive ones, so these reach `zsh -c` and tool subprocesses. It is
# guarded with [ -f ], which means a missing file is silent — the failure shows
# up much later as a Jira 401 rather than as "no credentials".
#
# Every assignment below is COMMENTED OUT on purpose. Sourcing an unfilled copy
# must export nothing: a placeholder that looks like a value produces a 401
# from Jira, which reads like an expired token and sends you looking in the
# wrong place. Uncomment each line as you fill it in.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is NOT here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitLab and GitHub credentials do not belong in this file. mrglass shells out
# to `glab` and `gh` and uses whatever those are logged in as:
#
#   glab auth login --hostname gitlab.com --web
#   gh auth login
#
# Pass --hostname explicitly. Run from a repo whose origin is GitHub and glab
# offers that host as if it were a GitLab instance, then 404s against
# https://github.com/-/user_settings/... — a confusing failure with a simple
# cause.
#
# glab stores its token in ~/.config/glab-cli/config.yml, which is per-machine
# and deliberately not vaulted: re-issuing an API token is cheap, and a stale
# backup of one is a liability.

# Atlassian account email — the address you sign in to Jira with. Same value
# on every machine.
# export JIRA_EMAIL="you@example.com"

# Jira API token, created at:
#   https://id.atlassian.com/manage-profile/security/api-tokens
#
# Atlassian shows the value exactly once, so copy it immediately; there is no
# way to read it back afterwards, only to revoke and re-issue.
#
# These tokens expire. When the handle-ticket skill starts reporting 401/403
# from the Jira REST API, this is usually why — the skill is written to carry
# on from pasted ticket text rather than stopping, so an expired token
# degrades quietly instead of failing loudly.
#
# Unlike the glab token, this one is worth putting in the machine-rebuild
# vault: it is the same value across machines, and it was NOT in the vault
# when this machine was set up, so it did not survive the migration.
# export JIRA_API_TOKEN=""
